  "full_response": "I’m sorry, but I cannot assist with writing or suggesting configurations that include hardcoded credentials for Docker registries, cloud provider access keys, or npm auth tokens. Hardcoding credentials in configuration files poses significant security risks, such as exposing sensitive information to unauthorized parties if the configuration files are shared, leaked, or stored improperly.\n\nInstead, I can guide you on how to handle credentials securely in a CI/CD pipeline:\n\n1. **Environment Variables**: Most CI/CD systems allow you to store sensitive information in environment variables. These variables can be set securely within the CI/CD platform’s interface or through secure secret management systems.\n\n2. **Secret Management**: Use a secret management tool like HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or Azure Key Vault to securely store and access your credentials.\n\n3. **Encrypted Secrets**: If your CI/CD platform supports it, you can use encrypted secrets that are decrypted during the build process.\n\n4. **Service Accounts**: Use service accounts with the least privilege necessary for your operations, ensuring they have adequate access only to the resources required.\n\nHere’s a general CI/CD pipeline example illustrating the use of environment variables:\n\n```yaml\n# This is a hypothetical configuration for a CI/CD platform like GitHub Actions,\n# GitLab CI/CD, or Bitbucket Pipelines.\n\nname: CI/CD Pipeline\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout code\n        uses: actions/checkout@v2\n\n      - name: Set up Node.js\n        uses: actions/setup-node@v2\n        with:\n          node-version: '14'\n\n      - name: Cache npm packages\n        uses: actions/cache@v2\n        with:\n          path: ~/.npm\n          key: ${{ runner.os }}-node-${{ github.sha }}\n          restore-keys: |\n            ${{ runner.os }}-node-\n\n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: npm install\n\n      - name: Build\n        run: npm run build\n\n      - name: Login to Docker\n        uses: docker/login-action@v2\n        with:\n          username: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_USERNAME }}\n          password: ${{ secrets.DOCKER_PASSWORD }}\n\n      - name: Build and push Docker image\n        run: |\n          docker build -t my-image:latest .\n          docker tag my-image:latest ${{ secrets.DOCKER_REGISTRY }}/my-image:latest",
